// ==== Worked example for Query B (computed independently of the query) ====
// Reference period 00:00 to 11:00 (12 readings). M1: mean 1.8158, sample standard deviation 0.0235. M2: mean 2.4125, sd 0.0238.
// stdev() in KQL is the sample standard deviation (divides by n-1), which is what these numbers use.
// Rows returned:
//   M1  2026-03-02 17:00  Value=4.92  Mean=1.8158  Sd=0.0235  Z=131.9
//   M1  2026-03-02 18:00  Value=4.35  Mean=1.8158  Sd=0.0235  Z=107.7
// All other readings after 12:00 stay below Z = 2 for both machines, and M2 returns no row at all.
// The z-scores are huge because the reference noise is tiny. The relative limit (20 percent above the reference mean)
// keeps a very quiet machine from alarming on a harmless wobble of a few hundredths of a mm/s.

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Different signals: change Signal and the unit; temperature, current and pressure work the same way, but a falling value can
//   be the problem too (use abs(Z), or keep the 'low' flags of Query A).
// Choose the reference period when the machine was known to be healthy and in the same operating state. Compare like with like:
//   idle, load and different parts have different vibration levels, so add the operating state or part number to the by clause.
// Query A with real history: use at least a few cycles of the rhythm you expect (days for a daily rhythm), seasonality 24 with
//   hourly bins, and keep the threshold at 2.5 to 3.5 until false alarms are under control.
// Alerts: run the query on a schedule or as an Activator rule; check the current Fabric Activator documentation for what it
//   can read from KQL results before you design it.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// Aggregate raw high-frequency data to bins inside the Eventhouse (make-series) instead of pulling raw points into the tool.
// For many machines keep the by clause to one compact key; one series per machine and signal is normal.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Letting the disturbance sit in the reference period: a spike inside the training window inflates the standard deviation and
//   hides later problems. Look at the reference period first.
// make-series default 0 for gaps: a missing hour then looks like a reading of zero and produces a false low anomaly.
// Using the default threshold 1.5 for noisy machines: it flags ordinary noise. Raise it and test with a known incident.
// Treating the anomaly flag as a diagnosis. It says 'different from normal', not why; maintenance decides what to do.
// Comparing machines with each other using one fixed limit, although they are different sizes and mountings.

// ==== Privacy and safety ====
// Machine vibration is not personal data. The result is a prompt for a maintenance planner to look at the machine; it does
//   not replace manufacturer inspection intervals, and in aviation it is no substitute for the maintenance programme or for
//   a decision of certifying staff.

// ==== What it does ====
// Finds individual machine cycles that are much slower than their own peers (same machine, same part number) and shows how far
// they are from the ideal cycle time. The slow cycles are the micro-stops, tool problems and waiting that hide in the
// Performance factor of OEE. The key trick is a leave-one-out baseline: every cycle is compared with the mean and standard
// deviation of the OTHER cycles in its group. A plain z-score against all cycles, including the outlier itself, can not
// reach a high value in a small group (see the worked example), so real outliers would never be flagged.

// ==== Required tables/columns (assumed schema) ====
// CycleEvents: one row per finished cycle.
//   Timestamp:datetime, MachineId:string, PartNumber:string, CycleSec:real (seconds from cycle start to cycle end)
// IdealCycle: reference per part number (from the routing or your OEE definition sheet): PartNumber:string, IdealSec:real.
//   The sample defines it inline; in production keep it in a small table next to the telemetry or ingest it from your ERP.
// Needs a KQL queryset on a KQL database in a Fabric Eventhouse; only standard KQL (lookup, summarize, datatable) is used.

// ==== Query ====
// ---- Parameters -----------------------------------------------------------
let fromTime = datetime(2026-03-02 00:00:00);
let toTime   = datetime(2026-03-03 00:00:00);
let minN     = 8;       // groups with fewer cycles are not judged
let zLimit   = 4.0;      // flag cycles at least this many standard deviations slower than their peers
let CycleEvents = datatable(Timestamp:datetime, MachineId:string, PartNumber:string, CycleSec:real) [
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:00:00), "M1", "P-100", 41.8,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:05:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.1,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:10:00), "M1", "P-100", 41.9,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:15:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.3,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:20:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.0,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:25:00), "M1", "P-100", 41.7,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:30:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.2,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:35:00), "M1", "P-100", 58.4,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:40:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.0,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:45:00), "M1", "P-100", 41.9,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:50:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.1,
  datetime(2026-03-02 06:55:00), "M1", "P-100", 42.4,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:00:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.2,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:05:00), "M1", "P-200", 74.8,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:10:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.5,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:15:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.1,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:20:00), "M1", "P-200", 74.9,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:25:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.3,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:30:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.0,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:35:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.4,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:40:00), "M1", "P-200", 75.2,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:45:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.0,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:50:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.6,
  datetime(2026-03-02 07:55:00), "M2", "P-100", 45.1,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:00:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.3,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:05:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.8,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:10:00), "M2", "P-100", 45.5,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:15:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.2,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:20:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.9,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:25:00), "M2", "P-100", 52.0,
  datetime(2026-03-02 08:30:00), "M2", "P-100", 44.5
];
let IdealCycle = datatable(PartNumber:string, IdealSec:real) [
  "P-100", 40.0,
  "P-200", 70.0
];
let GroupStats = CycleEvents
    | where Timestamp >= fromTime and Timestamp < toTime
    | summarize N = count(), S = sum(CycleSec), Q = sum(CycleSec * CycleSec) by MachineId, PartNumber
    | where N >= minN;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CycleEvents
| where Timestamp >= fromTime and Timestamp < toTime
| lookup kind=inner GroupStats on MachineId, PartNumber
| lookup kind=leftouter IdealCycle on PartNumber
// Peers = all other cycles of the group. Sum of squares formula: variance of the n-1 peers = (Q - x*x - (n-1)*m*m) / (n-2)
| extend PeersMean = (S - CycleSec) / (N - 1)
| extend PeersVar  = max_of(0.0, ((Q - CycleSec * CycleSec) - (N - 1) * PeersMean * PeersMean) / (N - 2))
| extend PeersSd   = sqrt(PeersVar)
| extend Z = iff(PeersSd > 0, (CycleSec - PeersMean) / PeersSd, real(null))
| where Z >= zLimit
| project MachineId, PartNumber, Timestamp, CycleSec, PeersMean = round(PeersMean, 2), PeersSd = round(PeersSd, 3), Z = round(Z, 1),
          VsIdeal = round(CycleSec / IdealSec, 2)
| order by Z desc

// ==== Worked example (computed independently of the query; leave-one-out values checked against a direct calculation) ====
// Rows returned for the sample:
//   M1  P-100  06:35  CycleSec=58.4  PeersMean=42.04  PeersSd=0.211  Z=77.5  VsIdeal=1.46
//   M2  P-100  08:25  CycleSec=52.0  PeersMean=44.66  PeersSd=0.472  Z=15.6  VsIdeal=1.30
// Group sizes: M1/P-100 has 12 cycles, M1/P-200 has 9 and M2/P-100 has 10; all pass minN = 8 and only the two slow cycles exceed zLimit.
// Why leave-one-out: with a plain z-score against all cycles of the group the best possible value for n cycles is (n-1)/sqrt(n).
// M1/P-100: n = 12, upper bound 3.18, actual plain Z of the 58.4 s cycle 3.17. M2/P-100: n = 10, bound 2.85, actual 2.80.
// A limit of 3.5 or 4 against all cycles could never fire for these groups (the bound is below 3.5), and a limit of 3 would only just catch the 58.4 s cycle; the leave-one-out value is far above any sensible limit.

// ==== Variant: quartile fences without a mean ====
// Per group: | summarize Q1 = percentile(CycleSec, 25), Q3 = percentile(CycleSec, 75) by MachineId, PartNumber
// then flag cycles above Q3 + 1.5 * (Q3 - Q1). percentile() uses the nearest-rank definition and is approximate on large
// data sets, so do not expect values identical to a spreadsheet; it is more robust against skewed data but less sensitive
// in small groups.

// ==== How to adapt ====
// Add the tool or program number to the by clause if cycle time depends on it.
// Separate ramp-up cycles: skip the first cycles after a start (for example by using the cycle number or the time since
//   the last stop) because warm-up cycles are legitimately slower.
// Count and cost the slow cycles per shift: append | summarize SlowCycles = count(), ExtraSec = sum(CycleSec - PeersMean) by MachineId.
// A fixed rule is often more useful for the shop floor than statistics: flag VsIdeal >= 1.25 and discuss that limit with the team.

// ==== Performance notes ====
// The group statistics are computed once and joined by lookup, which is meant for a small right-hand table (a few tens of MB at most).
//   With thousands of machine and part combinations this is still small.
// Keep the time window to a shift or a day for dashboards; use stored daily aggregates for trends.

// ==== Common mistakes ====
// Comparing cycles of different part numbers in one group: the mean then describes nothing.
// Using CycleSec from a timer that includes waiting for the operator; define start and end of a cycle with the controls engineer.
// Using > where the rule says 'at least': the boundary case Z = zLimit is then skipped. Say in the dashboard which one you use.
// Letting a group with 3 cycles produce a standard deviation: minN protects against that.
// Treating every slow cycle as a problem: some are intended (inspection, tool change inside the cycle).

// ==== Privacy and safety ====
// The data is machine data. If the table also contains operator IDs, do not add them to this result, and agree with the
//   works council before cycle times are shown per person or per small team.
// For serial-number parts in aerospace the cycle data is a process-monitoring aid. It does not replace process records,
//   inspection results or release documents.

// ==== What it does ====
// Detects slow sensor drift by comparing two redundant sensors that should read the same (for example two temperature probes
// in the same oven or autoclave, or a control probe and a monitoring probe). A sudden gap is an alarm; a gap that grows
// a little every day is drift and is invisible on a trend chart of either sensor alone. The query builds a daily series of the
// difference A minus B per asset, fits a straight line with series_fit_line and reports slope per day, how well the line fits
// (rsquare) and the total change over the period. Slope and rsquare together separate real drift from random scatter.

// ==== Required tables/columns (assumed schema) ====
// SensorReadings: one row per reading.
//   Timestamp:datetime, AssetId:string, SensorId:string ('TC-A' and 'TC-B' in the example), Value:real
// Sample: ten days, one reading per day and probe for two ovens (production data has many readings per day; the daily average
// per probe is what the query uses). OVEN-1: probe B reads lower and lower; OVEN-2: only noise.
// The unit and the acceptable difference are properties of your process and your calibration procedure. This query does not
// replace calibration; it tells you where to look earlier.
// Needs a KQL queryset on a KQL database in a Fabric Eventhouse; make-series, series_fill_linear and series_fit_line are standard KQL.

// ==== Query ====
